11) 25 COMMON VIVA QUESTIONS WITH SIMPLE ANSWERS
====================================================================
Q1. What is OwnQuesta in one line?
A: OwnQuesta is an AI-powered learning and AutoML platform for data science students.

Q2. Why did you choose this topic?
A: I observed students struggle in practical ML implementation, so I built a guided platform.

Q3. What problem does it solve?
A: It reduces the gap between ML theory and real implementation.

Q4. Why React + Next.js?
A: React gives reusable UI components and Next.js gives production-ready routing and app structure.

Q5. Why Node.js backend?
A: Node.js is efficient for API development and integrates well with frontend JavaScript stack.

Q6. Why FastAPI additionally?
A: AI/data workflows are Python-oriented, so FastAPI is better for those services.

Q7. Why not only Node.js for everything?
A: For AI execution and data-science workflows, Python ecosystem is stronger.

Q8. Which database did you use and why?
A: MongoDB Atlas, because it is managed, scalable, and good for JSON-like application data.

Q9. How is authentication handled?
A: Through backend auth APIs and session/auth middleware patterns.

Q10. How do services communicate?
A: Frontend calls backend/FastAPI endpoints through configured API URLs.

Q11. How is your app deployed?
A: Services are hosted on AWS EC2, data on MongoDB Atlas, and storage use-cases on S3.

Q12. What are major modules?
A: Auth, dashboard, AutoML/lab, admin monitoring, and AI-assisted workflows.

Q13. How do you handle file uploads?
A: FastAPI upload endpoint validates extensions and stores files in session-based temp paths.

Q14. How do you handle long code execution?
A: Execution has timeout control and process recovery for stability.

Q15. How is security handled?
A: Validation, restricted execution commands, route-level controls, and controlled API access.

Q16. Did you use third-party AI APIs?
A: Yes, I integrated OpenAI and Anthropic for agent capabilities.

Q17. What is your role in this project?
A: I worked on architecture, integration, implementation, and deployment flow.

Q18. What is the hardest part?
A: Reliable multi-service integration and keeping execution sessions stable.

Q19. What did you learn technically?
A: Full-stack architecture, API design, cloud deployment, and practical AI integration.

Q20. How is this relevant to data science?
A: It supports practical model workflow and AI-assisted learning for data science users.

Q21. How do you ensure scalability?
A: By separating frontend, backend, and AI services so each can scale independently.

Q22. What if one service fails?
A: Other services can continue, and health checks + restart strategy help recovery.

Q23. What improvements are planned?
A: Better model explainability, stronger MLOps automation, and personalized guidance.

Q24. Is this only academic or real-world useful?
A: It is practical and deployable, suitable for EdTech or ML-learning platform use.

Q25. Why should we grade this project highly?
A: It demonstrates complete product thinking: full-stack engineering, AI integration, cloud deployment, and data science relevance.

====================================================================
12) TOUGH QUESTIONS (EXTERNAL FACULTY) + STRONG ANSWERS
====================================================================
Q: How do you justify using both Next.js and Node backend?
A: Next.js handles UI and app-level web concerns, while dedicated Node backend centralizes core business APIs and auth logic for cleaner service boundaries.

Q: Why microservice-like split? Isn't it over-engineering?
A: The split is intentional because AI execution and standard web APIs have different runtime needs and scaling patterns.

Q: How do you avoid arbitrary code execution risks in lab service?
A: We added code restrictions, blocked dangerous patterns, execution timeouts, and isolated worker process handling.

Q: What are your measurable outcomes?
A: Reduced friction for students in moving from dataset upload to guided ML workflow and generated outputs, in a single platform.

Q: What if API keys fail or provider is down?
A: We designed layered integration and can add fallback behavior plus robust error messaging to keep user flow stable.

